Reasoning through the schema and ontology:

Entity types and identifiers:
1. Vessel — the ship itself. The schema is a vessel AIS feed, and mmsi is populated in every record and is the canonical AIS identifier, so mmsi is the subject key (expression vessel_{mmsi}). It maps to bsm:Vessel. imo, callsign, and ship_id are alternative identifiers for the same ship.
2. Alternate Identifier — the schema carries several identifiers under distinct schemes for the same vessel (MMSI, IMO, callsign, plus the source's internal ship_id). That is exactly the multi-scheme structure bsm:AlternateIdentifier models (standard code + value), so imo and callsign each become their own AlternateIdentifier entity, linked from the vessel via bsm:hasAlternateIdentifier. ship_id is instead the source system's own identifier, so it fits the literal bsm:sourceIdentifier on the vessel better than an AlternateIdentifier entity.
3. Observation — each stream record is a time-stamped position report, and the timestamp column directly evidences bsm:observationDateTime. The vessel links to it via bsm:hasObservation (declared on Vehicle). Keyed obs_{mmsi}_{timestamp} so each report is distinct.
4. Velocity — speed and course columns populate bsm:Velocity (speedMetersPerSecond, absoluteBearingAngle), reached via the unattached bsm:hasVelocity hung on the Observation. Keyed velocity_{mmsi}_{timestamp}.
5. Orientation — heading populates bsm:Orientation.headingAngle via bsm:hasOrientation. Keyed orientation_{mmsi}_{timestamp}.
6. Location and Absolute Point — lat/lon populate bsm:AbsolutePoint (latitudeDecimal, longitudeDecimal), which is the geometry of a bsm:Location via bsm:hasGeometry, reached from the Observation via bsm:hasGeographicLocation. Keyed location_{mmsi}_{timestamp} and point_{mmsi}_{timestamp}.
7. Geopolitical Affiliation — flag holds country codes (PL, LT), matching bsm:GeopoliticalAffiliation.geopoliticalAffiliationCode, linked from the vessel (an Object) via bsm:hasAffiliation. Keyed geoaff_{flag}.

Ports: the schema has rich port columns (last_port, current_port, next_port, unlocodes, destination, eta), and the ontology has bsm:Port, but no object property leads from Vessel, Observation, or any other reachable class to Port or Facility (hasControlledFacility belongs to Actor, and the vessel is not an actor). Since the relation could not be expressed without inventing a term, all port/destination/eta fields stay unmapped rather than creating floating entities.

Near-synonym disambiguations:
- conceptName vs alternateName: shipname is the vessel's primary name, so bsm:conceptName.
- typeName vs vesselCategoryCode: type_name holds text (Frigate, Submarine) matching "type name"; shiptype holds the numeric AIS code '35', and the qualifier "code" in vesselCategoryCode decides it.
- speedMetersPerSecond vs vesselMaximumSpeed: the qualifier "maximum" assigns max_speed to bsm:vesselMaximumSpeed; the current speed column goes to Velocity.speedMetersPerSecond, noting a unit caveat (AIS speed is knots scaled by 10, not m/s) that lowers confidence.
- headingAngle vs absoluteBearingAngle: heading maps to headingAngle (exact word); course (course over ground) maps to absoluteBearingAngle.
- status vs operatingModeCode: AIS navigational status '0' is a code for the vessel's current operating state; this is a genuine but weak match, kept at low confidence rather than defaulted confidently.
- ais_type_summary ('Military') has no dedicated code property (militaryCivilianCode is Aircraft-only), so it goes to the catch-all additionalInformationText at low confidence; market ('NAVAL') has no reasonable home and stays unmapped.

IRI consistency check: every iri-typed value's transformation string is copied character-for-character from the referenced entity's subject_transformation (obs_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, velocity_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, orientation_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, location_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, point_{mmsi}_{timestamp}, geoaff_{flag}, altid_imo_{imo}, altid_callsign_{callsign}). All class and property URIs were re-checked verbatim against the ontology. No empty transformation objects are emitted; literal values used as-is omit the key entirely.

